Chapter: ThrottleSpec
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter Pose
  cites Safari chapter Bike
  cites Safari chapter VehicleLimits
  cites Safari chapter World
  cites Safari chapter Arc
  cites Safari chapter Throttle
  cites Judge chapter Grade

 Throttle: how much the rider speeds up or slows down this frame, decided by a
 chain of gates that can only ever take MORE away.

 EVERY GATE IS A MINIMUM AND THAT IS THE SHAPE OF THE WHOLE CHAPTER. The corner
 brake, the pig gate and the shoulder brake each answer `min(their own, what came
 in)`, so a gate that does not apply is the identity and no ordering between them
 can matter. Grading each one in isolation with a large incoming acceleration and
 again with a small one is what says so -- a gate written as an assignment
 instead of a minimum passes the first and fails the second.

 THE CAT GATE IS THE ONE EXCEPTION AND IT IS NOT A MINIMUM. It zeroes a POSITIVE
 acceleration and leaves a negative one alone, because the rider stops closing on
 the cat but never brakes for it -- coasting in slows his approach, so he reaches
 the last segment later and there is more sunset. A min would have him braking.

 THE SPEED CLAMP RUNS LAST AND IS NOT AN ACCELERATION AT ALL: it clamps the
 resulting SPEED and the answer is the difference. So a rider already at the
 maximum gets zero however much acceleration the gates allowed, which is graded.

 We say:

Section: The Constants

  fixed-got : List Real
  fixed-got = [tilt-hold, brake-decay, tilt-hold / deg]

  fixed-want : List Real
  fixed-want = [0.03490658503988659, 20.0, 2.0]

Section: The Rider

  seg : Segment
  seg = segment-at 0

  rider-at : Real, Real, Real, Real -> RiderState
  rider-at (across) (along) (v) (tilt) =
    RiderState { segment = 0, along = along, across = across, yaw = 0.0, v = v,
                 tilt = tilt, heading = 0.0, gaze-yaw = 0.0, focus = 0.0 }

  rider : Real, Real, Real -> RiderState
  rider (along) (v) (tilt) = rider-at 0.0 along v tilt

Section: The Corner Brake

 A corner far off asks for a gentle slowing; one already reached asks for nothing
 at all rather than dividing by a distance of zero.

  corner-got : List Real
  corner-got =
    [ corner-brake (rider 498.4 0.3 0.0) seg 0.222 0.01,
      corner-brake (rider 600.0 0.3 0.0) seg 0.222 0.01,
      corner-brake (rider 0.0 0.3 0.0) seg 0.222 (0.0 - 5.0) ]

  corner-want : List Real
  corner-want = [0.0, 0.0, -5.0]

 It is a MINIMUM: an incoming acceleration already lower than the corner's is
 kept, and one higher is replaced. Both directions on the same corner.

  corner-min-got : List Boolean
  corner-min-got =
    [ corner-brake (rider 0.0 0.3 0.0) seg 0.222 0.01 <= 0.01,
      corner-brake (rider 0.0 0.3 0.0) seg 0.222 (0.0 - 5.0) <= (0.0 - 5.0),
      corner-brake (rider 0.0 2.0 0.0) seg 0.222 0.01 < corner-brake (rider 0.0 0.3 0.0) seg 0.222 0.01 ]

  corner-min-want : List Boolean
  corner-min-want = [True, True, True]

Section: The Cat Gate

 Segment 0 has no cat, so the gate is the identity whatever comes in. Segment 1
 does, and inside the danger window it zeroes a positive acceleration and leaves
 a negative one exactly as it was.

  cat-seg : Segment
  cat-seg = segment-at 1

  cat-got : List Real
  cat-got =
    [ cat-gate (rider 0.0 0.3 0.0) seg 0.01,
      cat-gate (rider 0.0 0.3 0.0) seg (0.0 - 5.0),
      cat-gate (rider 120.0 0.3 0.0) cat-seg 0.01,
      cat-gate (rider 120.0 0.3 0.0) cat-seg (0.0 - 5.0),
      cat-gate (rider 0.0 0.3 0.0) cat-seg 0.01 ]

  cat-want : List Real
  cat-want = [0.01, -5.0, 0.0, -5.0, 0.01]

Section: Staying On The Road

 Three outcomes, two of which are a departure.

  outcome : Shoulder -> ArcOutcome
  outcome (sh) =
    ArcOutcome { shoulder = sh, forward = 10.0, crossed = False, end-across = 0.0, frames = 40.0 }

  road-got : List Boolean
  road-got =
    [ stayed-on-road (outcome ShoulderNone),
      stayed-on-road (outcome ShoulderLeft),
      stayed-on-road (outcome ShoulderRight) ]

  road-want : List Boolean
  road-want = [True, False, False]

 THE FURTHER OFF THE SHOULDER, THE GENTLER THE BRAKE, and the decay is what makes
 a distant one almost free. A departure forty frames away asks for far less than
 one two frames away, and both ask for a slowing rather than a speeding.

  soon : ArcOutcome
  soon = ArcOutcome { shoulder = ShoulderLeft, forward = 10.0, crossed = False, end-across = 0.0, frames = 2.0 }

  brake-got : List Boolean
  brake-got =
    let far = shoulder-brake-at (rider 0.0 1.0 0.0) (outcome ShoulderLeft) 0.01
    in let near-off = shoulder-brake-at (rider 0.0 1.0 0.0) soon 0.01
    in let already = shoulder-brake-at (rider 0.0 1.0 0.0) (outcome ShoulderLeft) (0.0 - 9.0)
    in [far < 0.0, far > near-off, already < (0.0 - 8.999)]

  brake-want : List Boolean
  brake-want = [True, True, True]

 A frame count of zero does not divide by zero: the denominator is floored at one.

 THE OUTCOME IS BOUND ABOVE THE LIST AND NOT WRITTEN INLINE. An application whose
 arguments continue onto the next line is CDX1070 -- Cobblestone refuses it and
 our interpreter does not, so it ran green here and stopped the zig arm emitting
 at all. Same family as the `==` between Reals in RideFocalSpec: the gap is in
 our front end, and the three-arm run is what surfaces it.

  instant : ArcOutcome
  instant = ArcOutcome { shoulder = ShoulderLeft, forward = 0.0, crossed = False, end-across = 0.0, frames = 0.0 }

  zero-frames-got : List Boolean
  zero-frames-got = [shoulder-brake-at (rider 0.0 1.0 0.0) instant 0.01 < 0.0]

  zero-frames-want : List Boolean
  zero-frames-want = [True]

Section: The Speed Clamp

 It clamps the SPEED, not the acceleration: never above the maximum, never below
 zero, and never below the corner's entry speed while approaching one.

  clamp-got : List Real
  clamp-got =
    [ clamp-v (rider 0.0 0.3 0.0) seg 0.222 1.0 False,
      clamp-v (rider 0.0 0.3 0.0) seg 0.222 3.0 False,
      clamp-v (rider 0.0 0.3 0.0) seg 0.222 (0.0 - 1.0) False,
      clamp-v (rider 0.0 0.3 0.0) seg 0.222 0.1 True,
      clamp-v (rider 0.0 0.3 0.0) seg 0.222 0.5 True,
      clamp-v (rider 0.0 0.3 0.0) seg 0.222 0.1 False ]

  clamp-want : List Real
  clamp-want = [1.0, 2.5, 0.0, 0.222, 0.5, 0.1]

Section: The Whole Decision

 Straight, upright, far from the corner and nothing to look at: the plain
 acceleration. The answer is a DIFFERENCE of speeds rather than the acceleration
 itself -- `clamp-v ... (v + a) - v` -- so it carries one subtraction's rounding
 and is graded a step out from it.

  decide : Real, Real, Real -> Real
  decide (along) (v) (tilt) = get-forward-accel-decel (rider along v tilt) seg

  whole-got : List Real
  whole-got =
    [ decide 0.0 0.3 0.0,
      decide 0.0 v-max 0.0,
      decide 0.0 0.0 0.0 ]

  whole-want : List Real
  whole-want = [0.01, 0.0, 0.01]

 LEANING PAST THE HOLD ANGLE STOPS HIM ACCELERATING, which is the one gate above
 that is not a brake -- it sets the starting acceleration to zero rather than
 taking a minimum, and it is symmetric in the sign of the lean.

 TO SEE THE GATE ALONE, THE SHOULDER BRAKE HAS TO BE OUT OF THE WAY, and the
 chapter's own bounds-widening is how -- with a subtlety that cost a wrong guess.
 `project-arc` widens the road to wherever the rider ALREADY IS, which means the
 bound sits exactly on him: a rider a hundred metres off centre leaves on the
 very next frame if he drifts further out. He has to lean back TOWARD the road,
 and then the widened bound is a hundred metres behind him and nothing he does in
 twenty-five metres of progress can reach it.

 So these lean left from far right. The hold angle is symmetric in the sign, so
 it gates them exactly as it would a right-hand lean: at it and past it he gets
 nothing, under it the plain acceleration.

  wide : Real -> Real
  wide (tilt) = get-forward-accel-decel (rider-at 100.0 0.0 0.3 tilt) seg

  tilt-got : List Real
  tilt-got = [wide (0.0 - 0.001), wide (0.0 - tilt-hold), wide (0.0 - tilt-hold - 0.5)]

  tilt-want : List Real
  tilt-want = [0.01, 0.0, 0.0]

 A LEAN UNDER THE HOLD ANGLE CAN STILL BE BRAKED, and finding that out is what
 the gates composing means. Just under two degrees is not enough to stop him
 accelerating, but it IS enough to walk him off a four-metre lane inside the
 two-thousand-frame horizon -- so the shoulder brake takes over and the answer is
 a slowing, not the plain acceleration. A tenth of that lean leaves him on the
 road and he accelerates normally.

  compose-got : List Boolean
  compose-got =
    [ decide 0.0 0.3 (tilt-hold - 0.001) < 0.0,
      decide 0.0 0.3 0.001 > 0.0,
      decide 0.0 0.3 (0.0 - (tilt-hold - 0.001)) < 0.0 ]

  compose-want : List Boolean
  compose-want = [True, True, True]

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "th-fixed " fixed-got fixed-want 0.000000000001)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "th-corner" corner-got corner-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "th-cmin  " corner-min-got corner-min-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "th-cat   " cat-got cat-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "th-road  " road-got road-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "th-brake " brake-got brake-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "th-zero  " zero-frames-got zero-frames-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "th-clamp " clamp-got clamp-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "th-whole " whole-got whole-want 0.000000000001)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "th-tilt  " tilt-got tilt-want 0.000000000001)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "th-comp  " compose-got compose-want)
  end
